The round-trip, executed:

```go
type User struct {
    Name  string `json:"name"`
    Email string `json:"email"`
    Age   int    `json:"age,omitempty"`  // skip when 0
}

// Marshal: Go → JSON
u := User{Name: "Al", Email: "al@x.com"}
b, _ := json.Marshal(u)
// {"name":"Al","email":"al@x.com"} — Age omitted (omitempty, zero value) — verified

// Unmarshal: JSON → Go
var parsed User
json.Unmarshal(b, &parsed)
// parsed.Name == "Al", parsed.Email == "al@x.com" — verified
```

The struct tag is the name mapping: `json:"name"` says 'this Go field serializes as the JSON key \"name\".' Without a tag, the Go field name is used as-is. The options: `omitempty` (skip zero values), `-` (never serialize), `json:"name,string"` (encode as a JSON string even for numbers).

The streaming alternatives for large data: json.NewDecoder(reader).Decode(&v) (parse from a stream — HTTP bodies, files) and json.NewEncoder(writer).Encode(v) (write to a stream — HTTP responses). The batch forms (Marshal/Unmarshal) work on complete []byte — fine for everything that fits in memory.

The error cases every handler must handle: syntax error (bad JSON — `not json` → error, verified) and type mismatch (JSON string into Go int → error, verified). Both are the CLIENT's fault — 400 in an HTTP context, never 500.
